WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.125
Your heart is two pumps, working side by side.

2
00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:09.075
Each side has an upper chamber, the atrium, which receives blood.

3
00:00:09.525 --> 00:00:13.175
And a lower chamber, the ventricle, which pumps blood out.

4
00:00:13.625 --> 00:00:17.462
In diagrams, the heart’s right side is drawn on the left —

5
00:00:17.462 --> 00:00:19.700
as if you were facing the person.

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.830
Blood that is low in oxygen comes back from the body through the venae cavae,

7
00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.225
into the right atrium.

8
00:00:27.675 --> 00:00:31.675
It passes the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.

9
00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:36.550
The right ventricle pumps it up the pulmonary artery, to the lungs.

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.750
In the lungs it picks up oxygen and gets rid of carbon dioxide.

11
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:48.125
Oxygen-rich blood returns through the pulmonary veins into the left atrium.

12
00:00:48.575 --> 00:00:52.175
It passes the mitral valve into the left ventricle.

13
00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:55.038
The left ventricle has the thickest wall,

14
00:00:55.038 --> 00:00:59.175
because it must push blood through the aorta to the whole body.

15
00:01:00.075 --> 00:01:04.300
So blood passes through the heart twice on every trip around the body:

16
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.225
once to the lungs, and once to the body.

17
00:01:07.675 --> 00:01:12.950
Both sides contract together, and the valves keep blood moving in one direction.

18
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.350
Remember: arteries carry blood away from the heart, and veins carry it back.
